From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:25:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] wasm: optimize static execution and native value
 boundaries

Expose a native UTF-8 string writer to Static Hermes extern calls and
allow Hermes string values as native arguments. Snapshot own enumerable
entries without JavaScript pair-array allocation, preserving getter and
proxy order.

Construct decoded objects using bounded runtime-local shape templates and
dense arrays through native slots. Keep templates rooted and independent
of document values; use general own-property definitions for indexed or
duplicate keys and mutable dictionary shapes. Add native descriptor
snapshots that compare data, accessors, symbols, prototypes and flags
without constructing descriptor objects or invoking accessors.

Emit compiled regex literal bytecode in Static Hermes C output while
creating fresh RegExp objects and retaining named-group initialization.
Add selective static recognition of original Math methods, excluding
random, and lower numeric imul to unsigned modulo-32-bit multiplication.
Other operands retain JavaScript coercion through the builtin call.

Validate object isolation and GC roots, descriptor mutations and SameValue
semantics, regex identity and state, numeric conversions, preserved host
replacements, and emitted native helper calls.

Resolve native object layouts from borrowed key bytes, compare collisions by
key content, and evict individual least-recently-used layouts. Build immutable
classes directly and decode values through callbacks into initialized, rooted
object and array storage. Retain explicit fallbacks for wide, indexed and
duplicate-key objects and expose construction counters without allocation.

Return the original primitive for full-range string slices and scan unchanged
ASCII case conversions before allocating temporary UTF-16 storage. Cache string
prototype data-property slots in Static Hermes while preserving replacements,
accessor receivers and prototype mutations.

Cache computed own data-property reads by weak shape and interned key, with
bounded per-site storage and generic semantics for unsupported objects and
keys. Trace both weak class and symbol references, and initialize private-name
cache counts in every generated unit.

Inline guarded numeric typed-array reads and proven-number bit operations.
Preserve attachment and bounds checks, numeric coercion, NaN boxing, and generic
fallbacks for unsupported element kinds and keys. Include mirrored runtime
layouts in the Static Hermes ABI descriptor.

Expose numeric heap and cumulative GC statistics for opt-in execution
observation without JavaScript allocation or heap traversal. Validate normal
and small-C computed loads, GC invalidation, detached arrays, numeric edges,
and the native value adapter against host and Wasm builds.

Retain two receiver shapes at ordinary named-read sites and two shape/key
pairs per computed-cache set without enlarging the computed cache. Keep
weak roots, prototype validation, accessors and dictionary-mode fallbacks.
Add direct present-storage reads for ordinary arrays while retaining
inherited lookup for holes and unsupported keys.

Propagate known closure code through bounded parameter, phi and return
analysis while preserving the actual closure environment. Permit bounded
Static Hermes inlining of small field readers into try/catch callers when
a fresh object argument exposes allocation and field operations to existing
object promotion. Keep other backends' inlining policy unchanged.

Lower proven numeric abs, ceil, floor, trunc, round and binary min/max with
shared JavaScript rounding, NaN and signed-zero semantics. Fuse eligible
numeric string-index comparisons with one UTF-16 code unit; guard unknown
receivers and preserve prototype lookup, GC roots and exception cleanup.

Validate callback identity and environments, inlining growth, object
elimination, cache invalidation, indexed reads and numeric/string edges in
host and Wasm execution, including normal and small-C output. Retain the
existing native value-adapter and runtime ABI checks.
